eld, that's 2, and no one discarded, so total 2 accounted, 2 unseen \u2014 but unshown says 3, contradiction.\n\nUnshown B6:3 \u2014 means 3 remain in wall and unseen hands.\n\nSo total unseen: 3.\n\nIf I have one in hand and one in meld, that's 2 visible to me, so 2 accounted, 2 elsewhere \u2014 but 2+3=5 >4, impossible.\n\nTherefore, **I cannot have B6 in hand if it's in the meld**, because that would require at least two, but unshown count suggests only 3 unseen, so maximum one can be in my hand if one in meld.\n\nBut unshown B6:3 \u2014 so 3 out of 4 are still in circulation (wall or other hands), so only one is revealed.\n\nBut my meld has one B6 \u2014 so that's the revealed one.\n\nTherefore, I cannot have another B6 in hand.\n\nSimilarly for B7: unshown:2, and two discarded by previous player, so 2+2=4, so no B7 left in wall, and I cannot have B7 in hand.\n\nBut I claimed B7 to form the meld, so I used it in the meld.\n\nAnd I had B7 in hand before? Hand list says B7:1 \u2014 but if I used it in meld, it's gone.\n\nBut I didn't need to use it \u2014 I used B6 and B8 from hand, claimed B7.\n\nSo I still have my original B7.\n\nBut previous player discarded two B7s, so two are out.\n\nUnshown B7:2 \u2014 means two are still hidden.\n\nTotal: 4 copies.\n\nTwo discarded, so two left.\n\nIf I have one in hand, and one in meld (the claimed one), then two accounted for me, and two discarded \u2014 total 4, so unshown should be 0, but it's 2 \u2014 contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only way is: the claimed B7 is one of the discarded ones, so it's not \"unshown\".\n\n\"Unshown tiles\" likely means not yet drawn from the wall, i.e., still in the wall.\n\nDiscarded tiles are shown.\n\nSo for B7: total 4.\n- Discarded: previous player played B7 twice \u2014 so 2 shown in discard.\n- In my meld: one B7 claimed \u2014 so third shown.\n- One left in wall or hand \u2014 unshown:1? But given as 2.\n\nGiven unshown B7:2 \u2014 so 2 still in wall.\n\nBut 2 discarded + 1 in meld