\n\nBut **B8** is safer than B9 because B8 is middle tile.\n\nW2: early character, could be part of 1-2-3. Risky if someone needs it.\n\nJ2: Dragon \u2014 always somewhat risky, but no signs of dragon collection.\n\nBest discard: **J2** \u2014 single, no pair, no meld, and dragons are often sought, but no evidence anyone is building them.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **across player has no dragon melds**, next player has winds, previous has nothing.\n\nF4: we have two \u2014 best **pair candidate** among honors.\n\nActually, better to **keep F4s** as potential winning pair.\n\nSo between keeping J2 or F4 \u2014 **F4 pair is better**.\n\nTherefore, **discard J2**.\n\nAlternatively, **discard W2** \u2014 off-suit, lone tile.\n\nBut W2 might be used in 1-2-3 or 2-3-4.\n\nIs there a way to form a chow in Characters?\n\nWe have only W2 \u2014 very hard.\n\nAnd no other W tiles.\n\nSo W2 is deadwood.\n\nBut is it safer than J2?\n\nLikely yes \u2014 **W2 is a simple tile**, common, less likely to complete a dangerous hand.\n\nBut let's check frequency:\n\n- Has W2 been discarded? Not in histories.\n- T2, B2 have been played, but not W2.\n\nStill, no major red flags.\n\nBut consider: **if we keep W2**, we can never form a flush or honor-heavy hand. It\u2019s a liability.\n\nSame with J2.\n\nBut **F4 pair has more value** \u2014 can be used for Melded Hand, Last Tile, etc.\n\nMoreover, **Two Dragon Pungs (6 points)** requires two dragon pungs \u2014 we don\u2019t have even one.\n\nSo J2 pung is useless unless paired with other patterns.\n\nConclusion: **J2 and W2 are equally deadweight**.\n\nBut **F4 x2 is our only viable pair candidate**.\n\nSo we should **avoid breaking F4 pair**.\n\nTherefore, **do NOT play F4**.\n\nSo choose between: Play B6, B7, B8, B9, W2, J2.\n\nAmong B-tiles: all part of existing melds. Safe to discard since already exposed.\n\nStandard rule: **once a tile is used in an exposed meld, discarding duplicates is safe**.\n\nWe have:\n- B5: pung \u2192 played safely\n- B6, B7, B8: used in chow \u2192 so playing any of them is acceptable\n\nBut **B9** hasn\u2019t been used \u2014 so playing B9 reveals new info \u2014 slightly more dangerous.\n\nSimilarly, **playing B8** \u2014 could complete 7-8-9 for someone.\n\nBut **B6 is least dangerous** \u2014 it's sandwiched in middle.\n\nBut wait \u2014 someone might be doing **Lower Tiles** or **Lower Four** \u2014 B6 is outside.\n\nOr **Middle Tiles** \u2014 B6 fits.\n\nBut B6 is already known \u2014 we used it in chow.\n\nSame with B7, B8.\n\nSo all are semi-safe.\n\nBut **B9 is most dangerous** \u2014 terminal.\n\nSo avoid playing B9.\n\nBetween B6, B7, B8: **B8** is highest \u2014 could complete 7-8-9.\n\nB6 completes 5-6-7 or 6-7-8.\n\nWe already have 6-7-8 melded \u2014 so B6 is redundant.\n\nBut same logic applies.\n\nActually, **all are safe**.\n\nBut **best practice**: when breaking up an exposed chow, discard the **end tiles first**, keep middle.\n\nHere, we must discard one \u2014 so discard **B6 or B8**, not B7 (middle).\n\nB7 is most useful for opponents.\n\nSo **avoid playing B7**.\n\nSimilarly, **don\u2019t play B6** \u2014 it\u2019s needed for 5-6-7 or 6-7-8.\n\nWait \u2014 no: B6 is already exposed \u2014 known.\n\nBut **opponents may already have avoided discarding B5/B7**.\n\nBut we have B5 pung \u2014 so B5 is known.\n\nSo **B6 is safe**.\n\nBut to minimize risk, **play the tile least likely to complete a chow**.\n\n- B6: can complete 4-5-6, 5-6-7, 6-7-8\n- B7: 5-6-7, 6-7-8, 7-8-9\n- B8: 6-7-8, 7-8-9, 8-9-x (invalid)\n\nSo B7 appears in three possible chows \u2014 **most dangerous**\n\nB6 and B8 appear in two each.\n\nBut B6: 4-5-6, 5-6-7, 6-7-8 \u2192 three\n\nSame for B8: 6-7-8, 7-8-9, 8-9-1? No \u2014 only two valid.\n\nSo B6, B7, B8 all enable two or three chows.\n\nBut **since we already exposed [B6,B7,B8]**, any opponent who wanted those chows likely already has them or avoided.\n\nSo **all are reasonably safe**.\n\nBut **W2 is completely isolated** \u2014 no connection to anything.\n\nAnd we can't use it.\n\nSimilarly, **J2 is isolated**.\n\nSo better to discard **W2 or J2** than break up dot sequence.\n\nBut **we must keep a balanced hand**.\n\nHowever, **F4 pair is valuable** \u2014 so don\u2019t discard F4.\n\nSo final decision:\n\nDiscard **W2** \u2014 it's a single, off-suit, and not helping any pattern we\u2019re building.\n\nWe are focused on **Dots and Honors** \u2014 W2 is the only Character.\n\nRemoving it simplifies the hand.\n\nReason: W2 is a singleton in a different suit, not contributing to any potential flush or multi-chow pattern. Keeping it blocks flexibility. Discarding it improves hand coherence towards a Half Flush or honor-based pair win.\n\nAnswer: Play W2\n\nFinal:\n\nEvaluation: Among the options, W2 is a stray tile in a different suit, not part of any potential chow or pair. While B-tiles are in our main suit, discarding one is acceptable but risks aiding chows. J2 and F4 are better kept as honor pair candidates. B9 is a terminal and slightly dangerous. W2 offers no value and hinders suit purity.\n\nReason: W2 is isolated and impedes development of a coherent hand. Removing it focuses the hand on Dots and Honors, aligning with potential Half Flush or meld-based patterns. It is also less dangerous than terminal or middle-dot discards.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
