te from hand.**\n\nSo after Chi B7 and Peng B5, we have:\n\n- Meld 1: [B6, B7, B8] (exposed chow)\n- Meld 2: [B5, B5, B5] (exposed pung)\n\nThen your **hand tiles** should be the remaining 7 tiles before drawing.\n\nBut you have only 7 actions listed, and last was Peng B5, Play W1, Play W2 \u2014 so after forming two melds (3+3=6 tiles), you drew and discarded several times.\n\nCurrent hand: 14 - 2\u00d73 = 8 tiles? No \u2014 standard: after forming a meld, you discard one, so hand size remains 13 until winning.\n\nActually: start with 13 tiles.\n\nEach turn: draw one, discard one.\n\nWhen you form a meld via Chi/Peng/Gang: you take a tile from discard, add to hand, then form meld (set aside), then discard one.\n\nSo after Chi or Peng, you still end with 13 tiles (including the new meld).\n\nBut melds are displayed separately.\n\nSo your **current hand tiles** are the ones you currently hold, excluding the melds.\n\nYou have two melds: 3+3 = 6 tiles.\n\nTotal tiles in play for you: 13.\n\nSo hand should have 13 - 6 = 7 tiles.\n\nBut you list: [W9, B6, B7, B8, B9, F4, F4, J2] \u2192 8 tiles.\n\nContradiction.\n\nList: W9:1; B6:1; B7:1; B8:1; B9:1; F4:2; J2:1 \u2192 that's 1+1+1+1+1+2+1 = 8 tiles.\n\nImpossible.\n\nUnless... did you draw a tile?\n\nIt's your turn \u2014 so you must have just drawn a tile, making it 14 in hand temporarily, then must discard one.\n\nYes \u2014 that\u2019s the state.\n\nSo before drawing, you had 7 hand tiles + 6 meld tiles = 13.\n\nThen you drew one \u2192 now 8 in hand, 6 in melds \u2192 total 14.\n\nNow you must discard one.\n\nSo current hand: 8 tiles as listed.\n\nBut **B6, B7, B8** are in a chow meld \u2014 why are they in hand?\n\nAh \u2014 **no**: when you form a chow via \"Chi\", you expose the three tiles: the discarded tile and the two from your hand.\n\nSo once [B6,B7,B8] is formed by Chi, those tiles are **no longer in hand** \u2014 they are in the meld area.\n\nTherefore, you cannot have B6, B7, B8 in your hand now.\n\nSo the hand list must be incorrect or mislabeled.\n\nLooking at: \"Your hand tiles are: [W9: 1; B6: 1; B7: 1; B8: 1; B9: 1; F4: 2; J2: 1].\"\n\nThis must be a mistake.\n\nAfter Chi B7 to form [B6,B7,B8], those three tiles are exposed.\n\nSimilarly, Peng B5 to form [B5,B5,B5] \u2014 those three are exposed.\n\nSo your hand should consist of the remaining tiles after those two melds were formed and subsequent discards.\n\nFrom history:\n- You did: Chi B7 (to form [B6,B7,B8]), then played T9, J1, J3, T7, T7, then Peng B5, then Play W1, Play W2.\n\nAfter Chi B7, you had 13 tiles: the chow (3) + 10 others.\n\nThen you discarded T9, J1, J3, T7, T7 \u2014 five discards.\n\nThen Peng B5: you took a B5 discard, added to hand, formed [B5,B5,B5], then discarded W1.\n\nThen discarded W2.\n\nSo total discards after Chi: T9, J1, J3, T7, T7, W1, W2 \u2014 seven discards.\n\nAnd you drew seven times (one per turn).\n\nSo current hand should be the initial 10 minus 7 discards, plus the draws.\n\nBut we don't know initial hand.\n\nHowever, the **current hand tiles listed include B6, B7, B8**, which were used in the chow \u2014 so they should not be in hand.\n\nTherefore, the list \"Your hand tiles are: [W9: 1; B6: 1; B7: 1; B8: 1; B9: 1; F4: 2; J2: 1]\" is **incorrect** \u2014 likely, B6, B7, B8 should not be there.\n\nMore plausible: your current hand is [W9, B9, F4, F4, J2, and three other tiles drawn since then], but the system says these are your hand tiles.\n\nGiven the confusion, perhaps the system lists hand tiles including those just used? Unlikely.\n\nAlternative: maybe the chow [B6,B7,B8] is not using your B6,B7,B8 \u2014 but you contributed two, and the third was discarded.\n\nBut still, once contributed, they are exposed.\n\nI think there is a data error.\n\nBut since the valid actions include [Play B6, Play B7, Play B8], it implies that you currently hold them \u2014 which only makes sense if the meld [B6,B7,B8] is not using tiles from your hand, which is impossible.\n\nUnless the meld is not yours? No, \"Your melds are\".\n\nConclusion: **There is a inconsistency in the state description.**\n\nBut to proceed, I must trust the input.\n\nPossibility: after forming a meld, the game system still lists the tiles in hand for simplicity? But that would be misleading.\n\nAnother idea: perhaps \"Your hand tiles\" includes all tiles you possess, and \"melds\" are subsets? But then B6 appears in both hand and meld \u2014 double-counting.\n\nNot possible.\n\nBest resolution: **assume that the hand tiles listed are correct, and the melds are additional**, meaning you have multiple copies.\n\nFor example, there are 4 copies of each tile.\n\nSo:\n- You have a meld [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 uses one B6, one B7, one B8.\n- You also have in hand: B6:1, B7:1, B8:1 \u2014 so second copies.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nYes! There are 4 identical copies.\n\nSo you could have:\n- One B6 in meld, one B6 in hand.\n\nSimilarly for B7, B8.\n\nAnd unshown: B6:2 \u2192 so two more in wall or other hands.\n\nTotal: 1 (meld) + 1 (hand) + 2 (unshown) = 4 \u2014 correct.\n\nSimilarly, B7: meld 1, hand 1, unshown 2 \u2192 total 4.\n\nB8: same.\n\nB5: meld has three, unshown:0 \u2192 so the fourth B5 must be discarded or in another hand.\n\nYes, possible.\n\nSo you have:\n- In melds: B5\u00d73, B6\u00d71, B7\u00d71, B8\u00d71\n- In hand: W9, B6, B7, B8, B9, F4\u00d72, J2\n\nSo you have second copies of B6, B7, B8.\n\nThat changes everything.\n\nYou can still make another chow or even a kong.\n\nBut chows are only three tiles.\n\nHaving extra B6,B7,B8 allows for patterns like:\n- **Seven Pairs** \u2014 if you pair up, but you have no other pairs.\n- **Tile Hog** \u2014 if you keep four of one, but you only have two of each.\n\nBetter to discard one of the extras.\n\nNow evaluate:\n\n- **Play B6**: discard extra B6. B6 is a simple tile. Unshown:2 \u2014 so not rare. Low risk.\n- **Play B7**: similar.\n- **Play B8**: similar.\n- **Play B9**: terminal, unshown:2 \u2014 moderate risk.\n- **Play W9**: terminal, unshown:4 \u2014 very dangerous.\n- **Play J2**: dragon, unshown:4 \u2014 very dangerous.\n- **Play F4**: wind, unshown:4 \u2014 but not prevalent or seat wind \u2014 less valuable, but still, could complete a pung.\n\nBest choice: discard a safe simple tile.\n\nAmong B6, B7, B8 \u2014 which is safest?\n\nLook at other players' discards.\n\nPrevious player: discarded B7 three times (Play B7, Play B7, Play B7) \u2014 so likely does not want B7.\n\nNext player: discarded B9, B4, B2, etc. \u2014 no B7.\n\nAcross: discarded B1, B1, B9 \u2014 not B7.\n\nSo B7 is frequently discarded \u2014 likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, B6: previous player discarded B6? No \u2014 discarded B4, B2, B1, B5 \u2014 not B6.\n\nNext player discarded B4, B2 \u2014 not B6.\n\nAcross discarded B4 \u2014 not B6.\n\nB6 less commonly discarded.\n\nB8: no one has discarded B8 in history.\n\nSo B7 is most frequently discarded \u2014 therefore safest to play.\n\nAlso, we have extra B6, B7, B8 \u2014 any can be discarded.\n\nBut B7 has been thrown by previous player multiple times \u2014 strong signal it's safe.\n\nTherefore, **Play B7** is the best move.\n\nOther options:\n- W9: too dangerous.\n- J2: dragon, never discarded, high risk.\n- F4: North Wind, not valuable, but unshown:4 \u2014 still, better than nothing.\n\nFinal decision: **Play B7**\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid actions, discarding B7 is the safest because it has been frequently discarded by the previous player, indicating it is not being collected. Other options like W9, J2, or F4 are either terminals or honors and pose higher risks of feeding opponents' high-scoring patterns. Extra B6, B7, B8 are redundant given the existing chow meld.\n\nReason: B7 has been discarded three times by the previous player, suggesting it is safe to discard. Keeping extra simples like B7 offers little advantage, and removing it reduces risk while maintaining hand flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
